#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342763 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ca363e2-c7b7-5196-bd38-10bcc30123ce" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Velocity Vector of a Particle Moving in Two Dimensions at a Given Time"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "62d988ce-7055-5dab-854b-dbfcf9561136" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(2)=⟨3,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342765} {:answer/id #uuid "2b56afd4-dfbf-54fa-8b47-1951aaa8eee1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(2)=⟨\\frac{2}{3},3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342766} {:answer/id #uuid "42fa4738-f43d-5c85-b563-2e9ccede4609" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(2)=⟨\\frac{4}{3},3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342767} {:answer/id #uuid "054f8b35-37f5-5d0b-a90a-573aaa235537" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(2)=⟨1,3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342768} {:answer/id #uuid "32f50140-03af-5080-9774-aae2cd6586da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(2)=⟨\\frac{2}{3},2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342769}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2b56afd4-dfbf-54fa-8b47-1951aaa8eee1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(2)=⟨\\frac{2}{3},3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342766} :answer-field/id #uuid "cbe41e6f-133b-517c-85be-d9e88e35dd11" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342764}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "04e13fd6-9b6c-5320-b070-cd6f7b64eb13" :question/math-academy-id "q-121717" :question/problem "The position vector $r$ of a particle $P$ (relative to a fixed origin $O$) at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\nr=⟨\\sqrt{4t+1},3t-2⟩,\\,t>0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the velocity vector $v$ at time $t=2.$" :question/worked-solution "The particle's velocity vector is given by the derivative of its position vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{4t+1}),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t-2)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{4t+1}},3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the velocity when $t=2,$ we substitute into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(2) & =⟨\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{4(2)+1}},3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{9}},3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{2}{3},3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342770 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "646467d1-f764-5a3c-b03b-7d74e588611d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Speed of a Particle at a Given Time"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12435079-d19e-5ffc-8ae6-38b74b4da42f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342772} {:answer/id #uuid "16db51d7-5f3b-5f12-8b31-e7283fea8ee3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342773} {:answer/id #uuid "54caf6ed-b72d-55b8-9639-eb529ca5484b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342774} {:answer/id #uuid "566dc172-c0fd-5437-8058-aaa44298ad94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342775} {:answer/id #uuid "e53547ec-5b80-57a7-891b-5a8b778e6b01" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342776}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "566dc172-c0fd-5437-8058-aaa44298ad94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342775} :answer-field/id #uuid "d0962e7f-c716-569b-ac93-c27db645b4a0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342771}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b9e2e9d7-7206-54bd-8133-c43a9871dfd4" :question/math-academy-id "q-121730" :question/problem "The position vector $r$ of a particle $P$ (relative to a fixed origin $O$) at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\nr=⟨4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩,\\,t>0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the speed of the particle at time $t=\\pi .$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the velocity vector $v.$ Differentiating the position vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the velocity when $t=\\pi ,$ we substitute in the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(\\pi ) & =⟨4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi ,-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi ⟩=⟨-4,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the speed at $t=\\pi $ we calculate the magnitude of $v(\\pi ),$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|v(\\pi )| & =\\sqrt{(-4)^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16+0} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the speed when $t=\\pi $ is $4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342777 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "11a24997-1f1e-5968-a331-980ca14be2f6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Times at Which a Particle Is Stationary"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "605cd42b-68ff-5a62-baae-385cc923602a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342779} {:answer/id #uuid "654fb3e3-066c-545e-bfb3-78caee10856f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342780} {:answer/id #uuid "b9caacc1-b6dc-5bf1-9d97-3ee15fe5da83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342781} {:answer/id #uuid "fecc0fef-48e4-55e6-b47f-f2b91fd85fd1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The particle is never stationary" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342782} {:answer/id #uuid "75dda238-1f7d-5cf4-bd3c-e6e7fd3f6d6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342783}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "75dda238-1f7d-5cf4-bd3c-e6e7fd3f6d6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342783} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf77831e-6e85-50c8-8e8a-fe8956f617d8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342778}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e25e0179-be85-5825-a6d6-eefada53b4e2" :question/math-academy-id "q-121739" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves in the $x$-$y$ plane (relative to a fixed origin $O$) with position vector\n\n$$\nr=⟨{t}^{2}-6t,(t-3)^{5}⟩,\\,t\\in [0,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nCalculate the times at which the particle is stationary." :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the velocity vector $v.$ Differentiating the position vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-6t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}((t-3)^{5})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t-6,5(t-3)^{4}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2(t-3),5(t-3)^{4}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe particle is stationary when its velocity is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =0 \\\\ ⟨2(t-3),5(t-3)^{4}⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we need\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(t-3)=0\\, & ⇒\\,t=3, \\\\ 5(t-3)^{4}=0\\, & ⇒\\,t=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution $t=3$ is the only solution that satisfies both equations, so this is the only value of $t$ when the particle is stationary."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083564 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "646467d1-f764-5a3c-b03b-7d74e588611d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Speed of a Particle at a Given Time"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c4e07ad6-dd1c-5396-93fc-0d8ebf1b1419" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨3, 4⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080761} {:answer/id #uuid "e35b79df-6db2-56d9-b768-d7b0091c4441" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080762} {:answer/id #uuid "062d2bc3-b90e-5f7e-b8a8-5b4898aba0e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080763} {:answer/id #uuid "12a30ede-c880-5c04-a6e9-aaedb629a133" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080764} {:answer/id #uuid "39311bf4-5dfe-513b-9f48-b5f020d07d6a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080765}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "062d2bc3-b90e-5f7e-b8a8-5b4898aba0e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080763} :answer-field/id #uuid "df4ff21d-e63d-54ad-9276-cb579bb6fa5c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080760}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "33e1a317-247a-5469-95d3-fedba6493e14" :question/math-academy-id "q-31646" :question/problem "A particle $P$ is moving in the $x$-$y$ plane. Its position vector relative to a fixed origin $O$ is given by\n\n$$\nr=⟨{t}^{3}-2{t}^{2}-t,\\,2{t}^{2}-4t+2⟩,\\,t>0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the speed of the particle at time $t=2.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the velocity vector $v.$ Differentiating the position vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}-2{t}^{2}-t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{2}-4t+2)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3{t}^{2}-4t-1,4t-4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the velocity when $t=2,$ we substitute in the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(2) & =⟨3(2)^{2}-4(2)-1,4(2)-4⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the speed at $t=2$ we calculate the magnitude of $v(2),$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|v(2)| & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25} \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342784 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "11a24997-1f1e-5968-a331-980ca14be2f6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Times at Which a Particle Is Stationary"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7c776a09-e4d0-5965-af20-3e036e3b0fb9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342786}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7c776a09-e4d0-5965-af20-3e036e3b0fb9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342786} :answer-field/id #uuid "402cfa08-b520-55b1-9ae2-b86eff3f1e46" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342785}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "182941c7-4ee6-503e-a745-3ad3bae7e902" :question/math-academy-id "q-352611" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves in the $x$-$y$ plane (relative to a fixed origin $O$) with position vector\n\n$$\nr=⟨\\frac{3(t-2)^{2}}{t+1},\\frac{3(t-2)^{2}}{t+5}⟩,\\,t\\in [0,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nCalculate the times at which the particle is stationary.\n\nThe particle is stationary at $t=$  \n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the velocity vector $v.$ Differentiating the position vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{3(t-2)^{2}}{t+1}),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{3(t-2)^{2}}{t+5})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{3(t-2)(t+4)}{(t+1)^{2}},\\frac{3(t-2)(t+12)}{(t+5)^{2}}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe particle is stationary when its velocity is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =0 \\\\ ⟨\\frac{3(t-2)(t+4)}{(t+1)^{2}},\\frac{3(t-2)(t+12)}{(t+5)^{2}}⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we need\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3(t-2)(t+4)}{(t+1)^{2}}=0\\, & ⇒\\,3(t-2)(t+4)=0\\,⇒\\,t=2,-4, \\\\ \\frac{3(t-2)(t+12)}{(t+5)^{2}}=0\\, & ⇒\\,3(t-2)(t+12)=0\\,⇒\\,t=2,-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution $t=2$ is the only solution that satisfies both equations, so this is the only value of $t$ when the particle is stationary."}]}
